<h3>"DISK BALLOONS" BEHIND AIRPLANES</h3>

An aircraft does much the same thing as me and my balloons: it remains
aloft by throwing down a spinning region of mass.  This mass consists of
two long, thin, vortex-threads and the tubular regions of air which are
constrained to circulate around them. The balloons crudely represent the
separatrix of a vortex-pair: the cylindrical parcels of air which must 
move with closed streamlines. 
<br><p>

So, how do airplanes fly?  Real aircraft use "invisible disk-balloons" to
stay aloft.  The two rows of "invisible balloons" form a single, very
long, downwards-moving cylinder of air.  This single cylinder has
significant mass and carries a large momentum downwards.

